  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 AM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nRidging becomes maximized at around 1030mb this morning as mostly \nclear skies have allowed temperatures to plummet into the 20s across \nthe interior areas before dawn. As the ridging gives way to weak \nshortwave troughing today and tonight, expect cloud cover to build \nfrom the southwest this afternoon and evening as subtle WAA \ncontinues overhead. Thus, we can expect high temperatures to be a \ncouple of degrees warmer today when compared to yesterday, getting \ninto the 50s area-wide by this afternoon. While there is modest lift \nin the lower levels of the atmosphere with the shortwave passing \nthrough tonight into Sunday morning, with subsidence in the mid to \nupper levels, any rainfall we could see will be light (less than a \ntenth of an inch); as of the current forecast, rain chances across \nUpper Michigan range from 20 to 40%. Dry weather and warmer \nconditions are in store the rest of the day Sunday into Monday as \nridging once again builds back into the area, with highs increasing \ninto the lower 60s over the interior areas via strengthening WAA. \n\nMore active weather returns late Monday as a Colorado low becomes \nvertcially-stacked over the Plains before lifting into northern \nOntario Monday night into Tuesday (or later, if at all, depending on \nthe model solution). As this occurs, the warm front of the low looks \nto bring rainfall back over the area near the Wisconsin border late \nin the day on Monday before overspreading the rest of the U.P. \nMonday night. With some of the guidance (such as the Euro and \nCanadian suites) slowing down the progression of the low and pushing \nthe track closer to the area (further south and east than 24 hours \nago), we could see additional rainfall occur through the mid-week \nbefore the cold front of the low pushes through; with the differing \nsuites diverging on the timing of the cold front, confidence has \ndecreased a little on when the cold front will move through Upper \nMI; the earliest appears to be Tuesday and Tuesday night following \nthe GFS suite, whereas the slowest are the Canadian and European \nsuites which bring the cold front through Wednesday and Wednesday \nnight. Regardless, the latest NBM brings a 40 to 80% chance of up to \nan inch of rainfall across the area between Monday evening and \nWednesday morning, with the highest chances in the south and west. \n\nDepending on how the low tracks, we may see lake effect \nprecipitation behind it or dry conditions to end next week. Should \nthe low take a more southerly route and end up closer to the U.P., \nwe could see additional wrap-around/lake effect rain showers over \nthe north to northwest wind belts; there could even be a mix over to \nsnow over the far west near the end of the week if the latest \ndeterministic Euro is to be believed. That being said, if the track \nof the low is more to the north and west (like it was yesterday \nmorning and the current GFS solution), then I'd expect conditions to \nremain dry and for lake effect precipitation to miss our area. \nCurrently, leaning more towards the former option as the Great Lakes \nwill act as a source of latent heat release, allowing for localized \ntroughing to develop overhead and thus increasing chances of \ntrending the track of the low closer to the Upper Great Lakes. \nRegardless, expect the temperatures to remain well above normal for \nearly November as highs in the lower 60s are expected Monday and \nTuesday and the 50s Wednesday and Thursday; the NBM has the lows \ngenerally in the 40s throughout much of next week.",
